//? Title: FNV-1a: byte updates, buckets and the low-bit trap
//? Subtitle: Exact native traces, controlled counterexamples and Titanic passenger names
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A hash table must turn byte keys into bucket indices, but a compact hash value
does not by itself guarantee balanced buckets. This report follows a two-byte
FNV-1a calculation, reproduces the original 32-word experiment, and derives a
family of distinct keys that all enter one low-bit bucket. Exhaustive one-byte
controls and Titanic passenger names separate integer-hash collisions, bucket
collisions and duplicated records. A final sensitivity experiment shows why a
few changed-bit counts cannot establish cryptographic diffusion. Every number
and figure comes from the canonical native hash loop or a reusable native
analysis helper.
)

\toc

> Two bytes, two actual updates

Start with the 32-bit offset basis and consume the two bytes of ASCII `ab`, 97
and 98. Each step first XORs its byte into the accumulator and then multiplies
by an odd prime modulo the word range. The records below are captured by the
same loop that computes the ordinary hash; they are not a second implementation
of the algorithm. Hexadecimal columns preserve every bit without conversion
through a floating-point number.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fnv_states.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    dm::mkdir("build");
    const auto trace = dm::algorithms::fnv1a_trace("ab", 32);
    std::print("{}\n", dm::algorithms::fnv_trace_table(trace).to_md());
    dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65, 1000);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 5.4;
    style.height = 2.6;
    style.font_size = 20;
    for (const auto &e : trace.steps) {
        diagram.node(std::format("s{}", e.index), {8.0 * e.index, 0},
                     std::format("Byte {}: {}\nBefore  {:08X}\nXOR     {:08X}\nAfter   {:08X}",
                                 e.index, e.byte, e.before, e.xored, e.after),
                     style);
        if (e.index) diagram.edge("s0", "s1", "carry state");
    }
    diagram.text({4, 2.3}, "XOR the byte, then multiply modulo 2^32", 24).text(
        {4, -2.3}, std::format("Final integer hash: {:08X}", trace.value), 24).save_svg("build/fnv_states.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| index | byte | before | xored | after |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 0 | 97 | 811C9DC5 | 811C9DA4 | E40C292C |
| 1 | 98 | E40C292C | E40C294E | 4D2505CA |

\image(figures/fnv_states.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual accumulator states for `ab`. Each complete record contains its byte index, unsigned byte and before/XOR/after values.)
\alttext(Two connected boxes show the accumulator before and after consuming bytes 97 and 98. The second box starts from the first box's final state. A table prints the same exact hexadecimal values.)

> The method and its exact arithmetic

For word width w, offset basis H0 and odd prime P, the update is

$$
H_{i+1}=((H_i\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}b_i)P)\bmod 2^w.
$$
\alttext(The next accumulator is the current accumulator XOR the next byte, multiplied by the FNV prime and reduced modulo two to the word width.)

The native 32-bit interface uses basis 2166136261 and prime 16777619; the 64-bit
interface uses basis 14695981039346656037 and prime 1099511628211. Bytes are
unsigned, embedded NULs are included, and no terminator is added. These are
integer interfaces. Displaying an integer in hexadecimal is distinct from
serializing the digest as the little-endian byte vector specified by RFC 9923,
Section 2.3.

An n-byte hash performs n XOR updates and n modular multiplications. These
selected operations do not count loop control, indexing, allocation, comparisons
in a surrounding hash table, or CPU instructions. At fixed word width, hashing
takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary state. Retaining one complete byte record per
update instead takes O(n) space.

Because P is odd, gcd(P,2^w)=1 and multiplication by P has a modular inverse.
For a *fixed byte*, the state update is a permutation. Consequently the 256
possible one-byte inputs have distinct full-width hashes. This does not make
hashes of arbitrary strings collision-free: more than 2^w possible keys cannot
fit injectively into 2^w values.

> The original 32 words: measure, do not certify uniformity

The original word list is retained in its original order. Its bucket rule takes
the lowest four bits of the 64-bit integer hash, which is equivalent to reducing
modulo 16. The two reference values are also recomputed. The word list is a
fixed fixture, not an independent random sample.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fnv_words.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    dm::mkdir("build");
    std::print("Empty 32-bit hash {:08X}; ASCII a {:08X}.\n\n",
               dm::algorithms::fnv1a_32(""), dm::algorithms::fnv1a_32("a"));
    const auto profile =
        dm::algorithms::fnv_bucket_profile(dm::algorithms::fnv_example_words());
    std::print("{}\n", profile.table().to_md());
    std::print("{}\n",
               profile.summary().select({"keys", "occupied", "max_load",
                                         "mean_load", "bucket_pairs",
                                         "hash_pairs"}).to_md());
    profile.plot().size(900, 560).save_svg("build/fnv_words.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Empty 32-bit hash 811C9DC5; ASCII a E40C292C.

| bucket | keys |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 2 |
| 2 | 1 |
| 3 | 1 |
| 4 | 0 |
| 5 | 4 |
| 6 | 1 |
| 7 | 2 |
| 8 | 1 |
| 9 | 2 |
| 10 | 3 |
| 11 | 2 |
| 12 | 3 |
| 13 | 0 |
| 14 | 2 |
| 15 | 7 |

| keys | occupied | max_load | mean_load | bucket_pairs | hash_pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 32 | 14 | 7 | 2 | 38 | 0 |

\image(figures/fnv_words.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Observed counts for the unchanged 32-word fixture. The horizontal line is the arithmetic mean of two records per bucket, not an acceptance band.)
\alttext(Sixteen bars show the actual bucket loads. Two buckets are empty and the most populated holds seven keys; a separate table distinguishes bucket pairs from full-hash collisions.)

For loads n0 through nB−1, the number of unordered record pairs sharing a bucket
is

$$
C=\sum_{j=0}^{B-1}\binom{n_j}{2}.
\qquad
\mathbb E[C]=\frac{n(n-1)}{2B}\quad\text{under independent uniform assignments}.
$$
\alttext(Observed bucket collisions count all unordered record pairs in each bucket. Under an explicitly independent uniform assignment model, each of the n choose two pairs shares a bucket with probability one over B.)

The expectation follows by summing one indicator per pair. It is a reference
model, not a theorem that FNV hashes this fixture uniformly. In particular, the
original report's near-uniformity conclusion is stronger than its experiment
supports. Even perfect balance for one fixture would not establish performance
on another distribution or resilience to chosen keys.

> Checking the theory: a complete one-byte census and a low-bit counterexample

Modulo 16, both native bases are 5 and both primes are 3. For a one-byte key b,

$$
H_1\bmod16=3(5\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}(b\bmod16))\bmod16.
$$
\alttext(The low nibble of a one-byte hash is three times five XOR the byte's low nibble, reduced modulo sixteen.)

All 16 byte values whose low nibble is 1 therefore enter bucket 12. Their
full-width hashes are distinct, but they produce 120 bucket-collision pairs.
Conversely, enumerating *all* 256 byte values gives exactly 16 records in every
bucket, since the low-nibble transformation is a permutation. Both results are
properties of these controlled input sets; neither implies universal balance.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fnv_low_bits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    dm::mkdir("build");
    const auto all =
        dm::algorithms::fnv_bucket_profile(dm::algorithms::fnv_byte_keys());
    const auto low =
        dm::algorithms::fnv_bucket_profile(dm::algorithms::fnv_byte_keys(4, 1));
    std::print("All 256 bytes:\n\n{}\nSame low nibble:\n\n{}\n",
               all.summary().select({"keys", "occupied", "max_load",
                                     "bucket_pairs", "hash_pairs"}).to_md(),
               low.summary().select({"keys", "occupied", "max_load",
                                     "bucket_pairs", "hash_pairs"}).to_md());
    auto left = all.plot(), right = low.plot();
    left.title("All 256 one-byte keys").y_limits(0, 23);
    right.title("16 keys with low nibble 1").y_limits(0, 23);
    dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 500).title("Balanced enumeration versus concentrated low-bit keys").fit_panels().save_svg("build/fnv_low_bits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
All 256 bytes:

| keys | occupied | max_load | bucket_pairs | hash_pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 256 | 16 | 16 | 1920 | 0 |

Same low nibble:

| keys | occupied | max_load | bucket_pairs | hash_pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 16 | 1 | 16 | 120 | 0 |

\image(figures/fnv_low_bits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The same 64-bit hash and the same modulo-16 reduction give radically different occupancies. Both input sets have zero full-hash collisions.)
\alttext(The left panel has sixteen equal bars of height sixteen. The right panel puts all sixteen distinct keys into bucket twelve. Identical vertical scales make their different occupancy patterns directly comparable.)

More generally, reduction modulo 2^k depends only on the corresponding low k
bits at each update. An input bit at position k or higher cannot change this low
residue. For the least significant bit, the especially simple identity is

$$
(H_n)_0=(H_0)_0\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}(b_0)_0\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}\cdots\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}(b_{n-1})_0.
$$
\alttext(The final low output bit is the initial low bit XOR the low bit of every input byte. Higher input bits do not participate.)

This explains the counterexample without a statistical argument. RFC 9923
discusses diffusion limitations and XOR folding; this report measures the
existing raw-modulo interface and does not claim that folding solves every
distribution problem.

> A dataset example: Titanic passenger names

The bundled canonical Titanic dataset supplies passenger names as byte keys. The
analysis uses the `Name` column directly: no name normalization, tokenization or
locale-dependent case conversion is introduced. Records with duplicated names
would remain separate bucket records while contributing only one distinct key to
the full-hash collision census.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fnv_titanic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    dm::mkdir("build");
    const auto data = dm::datasets::titanic();
    if (data.null_count("Name"))
        throw std::runtime_error("Missing passenger names require an explicit policy");
    const auto &names = data.get_column<std::string>("Name").values();
    const auto p32 = dm::algorithms::fnv_bucket_profile(names, 64, 32);
    const auto p64 = dm::algorithms::fnv_bucket_profile(names, 64, 64);
    std::print("Dataset: {} rows, {} columns; {} distinct names; {} hashed bytes.\n\n",
               data.nrows(), data.ncols(), p64.distinct_keys, p64.bytes);
    std::print("32-bit hashes:\n\n{}\n64-bit hashes:\n\n{}\n",
               p32.summary().select({"keys", "occupied", "max_load",
                                     "bucket_pairs", "hash_pairs"}).to_md(),
               p64.summary().select({"keys", "occupied", "max_load",
                                     "bucket_pairs", "hash_pairs"}).to_md());
    p64.plot().size(900, 560).save_svg("build/fnv_titanic.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Dataset: 891 rows, 12 columns; 891 distinct names; 24026 hashed bytes.

32-bit hashes:

| keys | occupied | max_load | bucket_pairs | hash_pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 891 | 64 | 22 | 6164 | 0 |

64-bit hashes:

| keys | occupied | max_load | bucket_pairs | hash_pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 891 | 64 | 24 | 6188 | 0 |

\image(figures/fnv_titanic.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual 64-bucket occupancies for the bundled Titanic names. The table separately measures the 32-bit and 64-bit full-hash interfaces.)
\alttext(A numeric bucket axis shows sixty-four observed passenger-name loads. The table reports occupied buckets, the largest observed load, record-pair bucket collisions and distinct-key full-hash collisions.)

A zero observed full-hash collision count is a finite census result, not a
guarantee for future names. The bucket load distribution matters to a particular
table implementation, but this experiment does not measure equality-comparison
costs, resizing, cache effects or lookup times. It cannot establish a universal
speed ranking.

> Sensitivity, actual collisions and complete-record limits

The following experiment flips each of the 40 bits in ASCII `hello` once and
counts changed output bits. Half the 64 output bits is an idealized reference
line, not a quality threshold. Late high input bits have limited routes into low
output bits, as the preceding modular argument predicts. A separate explicit
pair, `costarring` and `liquid`, demonstrates a full 32-bit collision. Adding a
duplicate of the first key tests the distinction between distinct keys and
records.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fnv_sensitivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    dm::mkdir("build");
    const auto flips = dm::algorithms::fnv_bit_flips("hello");
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(flips.get_column<double>("input_bit").values(),
              flips.get_column<double>("changed_output_bits").values(),
              "bit flips", {37, 99, 235});
    plot.abline_h(32, {220, 38, 38}, 1.5).title("One-bit changes to ASCII hello").x_label("input bit (byte-major, least significant first)").y_label("changed output bits").y_limits(0, 64).size(900, 560).save_svg("build/fnv_sensitivity.svg");
    const auto stats = flips.describe_numeric("changed_output_bits");
    std::print("{} flips; mean changed bits {:.3f}; range {:.0f} to {:.0f}.\n\n",
               flips.nrows(), stats.mean, stats.min, stats.max);
    const auto collision =
        dm::algorithms::fnv_bucket_profile({"costarring", "liquid", "costarring"},
                                           16, 32);
    std::print("{}\n",
               collision.summary().select({"keys", "distinct_keys",
                                           "bucket_pairs", "hash_pairs"}).to_md());
    std::print("The two distinct keys have 32-bit hashes {:08X} and {:08X}.\n\n",
               dm::algorithms::fnv1a_32("costarring"),
               dm::algorithms::fnv1a_32("liquid"));
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::fnv1a_trace("ab", 32, 1);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        std::print("A one-record quota rejects the complete two-byte trace.\n\n");
    }
    std::print("Admitted records: {}; empty-input records: {}; embedded-NUL records: {}.\n",
               dm::algorithms::fnv1a_trace("ab", 32, 2).steps.size(),
               dm::algorithms::fnv1a_trace("", 32).steps.size(),
               dm::algorithms::fnv1a_trace(std::string("a\0", 2), 32).steps.size());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
40 flips; mean changed bits 22.250; range 7 to 39.

| keys | distinct_keys | bucket_pairs | hash_pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 3 | 2 | 3 | 1 |

The two distinct keys have 32-bit hashes 5E4DAA9D and 5E4DAA9D.

A one-record quota rejects the complete two-byte trace.

Admitted records: 2; empty-input records: 0; embedded-NUL records: 2.
\image(figures/fnv_sensitivity.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The sensitivity curve is a finite 40-perturbation experiment. The separate collision table counts three records, two distinct keys, three bucket pairs and one distinct-key full-hash pair.)
\alttext(A curve of changed output-bit counts varies by flipped input position; a red line marks thirty-two bits. Printed controls retain a real 32-bit collision, duplicate-key accounting, an admitted two-record trace and rejection of an insufficient record quota.)

> Conclusion

The native ordinary hashes retain their byte-for-byte behavior while their
shared trace exposes every actual update. Exact enumeration can look perfectly
balanced, yet a different family of distinct keys can collapse into one low-bit
bucket. The original word fixture and the Titanic names therefore support
statements about their measured occupancies only. A reproducible full-width
collision and the algebraic low-bit dependency show why FNV must not be treated
as a cryptographic primitive.

Trace quotas count complete byte records, not bytes of memory or peak process
RAM. Ordinary hashing remains constant-state; bucket census helpers retain
counts and temporary dictionaries, and the controlled bit-flip experiment
intentionally hashes multiple copies. These costs are separate from the cost of
one native hash call.

> References and implementation scope

- L. Noll, K. Vo, D. Eastlake 3rd and T. Hansen (2026), *The FNV
  Non-Cryptographic Hash Algorithm*, RFC 9923. Sections 2 and 2.3 define the
  update and integer/byte serialization distinction; Sections 1.4 and 6 discuss
  limitations. https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9923.html
- Canonical implementation: `include/datamunge/algorithms/fnv_hash.hpp`. Native
  report helpers: `include/datamunge/algorithms/fnv_analysis.hpp`.
